0.119 2026-09-21
 - fixed a real, currently-latent defensive gap: Browser::CLI's main() and main_search() both called encode_json($result) directly for their final successful-result formatting, OUTSIDE the eval wrapper _run_and_report_errors already provides for the executor call itself - every other failure path in this file goes through _sanitize_error() and this project's consistent exit-code-2 convention, but this one final formatting step was unprotected. encode_json can itself die (e.g. on a circular reference, live-verified via a throwaway perl -MJSON::PP invocation), and that die would have propagated uncaught from main()/main_search(), breaking this codebase's own clean-error convention with a raw internal Perl/JSON::PP message and exit 255 instead. Extracted a shared _print_json_result_or_report_error helper that wraps encode_json in its own eval and routes any failure through the same _sanitize_error/exit-2 path, used by both main() and main_search(). This is currently unreachable with today's result shapes (Browser::Runner/Browser::Search never produce circular structures), so no live crash was being fixed - closing a genuine inconsistency before it could ever matter, exactly analogous to this session's own D2B-133/134/135 chain. Full TDD cycle (red/green - the red test forces main()'s runner to return a genuinely circular result, and main_search()'s underlying execute_search is locally overridden to return one too, since Browser::Search's own public API has no way to inject a circular parsed result; a regression-guard block confirms a normal, encodable result is completely unaffected). Found by a scheduled hunt targeting cli/ wrappers and CLI.pm's error-handling consistency (D2B-139)

0.118 2026-09-21
 - documented a narrow, real edge case: browser.post --data --help/--data --version, passed as two SEPARATE arguments, is misinterpreted as a help/version request instead of being sent as the literal POST body, because Browser::CLI's pre-parse --help/--version detection scans the whole raw argument list for a standalone matching element before Getopt::Long ever assigns --data its value - a deliberate, already-documented design (D2B-096/D2B-124) favoring reliable --help/--version detection even alongside a broken sibling flag. The combined equals-form (--data=--help/--data=--version) is unaffected, since that's a single argv token rather than a standalone matching element - a distinction a code-review round caught was missing from the first documentation draft, which overclaimed the limitation applied to --data=--help too. Added a clarifying, correctly-scoped note to README.md, docs/usage.md, and SKILLS.md's --data documentation, plus a new t/ test that both checks the doc wording and directly exercises _argv_requests_help/_argv_requests_version to confirm the space-form/equals-form distinction behaviorally, not just in prose. No code behavior changed - changing the pre-parse scanning logic itself is a real design decision deserving its own deliberate ticket if ever revisited. Found by a scheduled hunt (D2B-138)

0.117 2026-09-21
 - fixed a real doc gap: browser.post's --data was never documented as going out with no Content-Type header explicitly set - _run_post always sends it as a raw string with no headers option of its own, and this skill has no --header flag anywhere for a caller to set one themselves. Every documented --data example ('--data name=dashboard') visually resembles a form submission, which could mislead a reader into assuming form-encoding happens automatically; a server that relies on Content-Type to parse the body may not interpret it as intended. Added a clarifying note to README.md, docs/usage.md, and SKILLS.md's --data documentation, plus a new t/ test asserting all three carry it. No code behavior changed - deliberately scoped as a doc fix rather than bundling in a request-behavior change (auto-setting Content-Type, or adding a --header flag), which is a real design decision deserving its own deliberate ticket. Found by a scheduled hunt (D2B-137)

0.116 2026-09-21
